@meta
id: 10-06
title: You do not have to be inside it
chapter: 10
type: lesson
skills: obj-quote
par: 97
minutes: 6
vscode: click into the string first, then select it
open: order.mailer.ts

@brief
The quote objects have a property the word objects do not: if the cursor
is not inside a string, vim looks forward along the line and uses the next
one it finds. From the first column of a line, `ci"` changes the first
string on that line. You never have to travel to it.

That single detail is what makes string editing in vim feel like cheating.
Jump to the line, press three keys, type.

Marketing have renamed the shipped notification. Three things change in
this mailer:

  - the subject line should read `Order ${order.id} has shipped`
  - the event name sent to the email service becomes `order-dispatched`
  - the template becomes `dispatched-html`

The `${order.id}` interpolation stays in the subject. Everything else in
the file is fine.

@keys
| `ci"` | change the next double-quoted string on the line |
| `ci'` | the same for single quotes |
| ``ci` `` | and for a template literal |
| `0` | first column, so the whole line is ahead of you |

@hints
Two of these strings are single-quoted and one is a backtick template.
The object you want is named after the quote character itself.
--
You do not need to steer the cursor into the string. From the start of the
line, `ci'` finds the first single-quoted string ahead of it.
--
`0` then `ci'` is a reliable pair: go to column one, change the first
string on the line. For the subject line the object is `` i` ``, and the
`${order.id}` has to be typed back in by hand.

@solution
    2j ci` Order ${order.id} has shipped <Esc>
    2j 0 ci' order-dispatched <Esc>
    2j 0 ci' dispatched-html <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Eighty-four keystrokes and sixty of them are the new text, which is the
sign of an edit done well: you paid for the words and almost nothing for
the navigation.

The forward search only goes as far as the end of the line, and it only
happens when the cursor is not already inside a string. Inside one, the
object is the one you are in. Both behaviours are what you want, and
together they mean the sequence `0ci'` works on any line with exactly one
string on it — which is most lines.

When a line has two strings, `0` gets you the first and `f` onto the
second one gets you the other. You saw that in the last lesson.

One warning: vim decides where a string starts by counting quotes from the
beginning of the line, so an apostrophe in a comment on the same line
will confuse `ci'`. Double quotes and backticks rarely have that problem,
and `u` costs one key.

@start order.mailer.ts
export class OrderMailer {
  public async sendShipped(order: Order, customer: Customer) {
    const subject = `Order ${order.id} is on its way`;

    return this.emailService.send(customer.email, 'order-shipped', {
      subject,
      template: 'shipped-html',
    });
  }
}

@expected order.mailer.ts
export class OrderMailer {
  public async sendShipped(order: Order, customer: Customer) {
    const subject = `Order ${order.id} has shipped`;

    return this.emailService.send(customer.email, 'order-dispatched', {
      subject,
      template: 'dispatched-html',
    });
  }
}
